 ## Overview
-Single Scattering Volume Rendering with Ray Marching is a GPU-based algorithm that computes volumetric light-shafts (god rays) in participating media by assuming light scatters only once before reaching the camera [S1][S4, unofficial]. It solves the radiative transport equation by ignoring multiple scattering, allowing the in-scattering term to be computed independently of unknown radiance [S1]. Unlike screen-space radial blur methods, this technique handles multiple light sources, colored fog, varying density, and physically correct behavior from any viewing angle, though it is more computationally expensive [S3, unofficial].
